WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:07.890
This mural is one of thousands in Philadelphia.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:14.070
They’re in yuppie Rittenhouse Square
and working class Fishtown,

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:18.640
near the Liberty Bell,
and in West Philly too.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.619
Why does Philly have so many murals?

00:00:21.619 --> 00:00:23.540
And how did they make it happen?

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:27.279
It’s not just because they’re pretty,
though they are that.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:33.540
Murals aren’t paintings, but tools….that
create wealth, employ people, and can help

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:44.829
reimagine any city, one wall at a time.

00:00:44.829 --> 00:00:49.010
At Broad and Spring Garden you’ll find
Meg Saligman’s “Common Threads.”

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.609
Across the street from that, near a grate,
you’ll find this humble pole.

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:58.359
This is kind of the thing that started Philly’s
obsession with murals.

00:00:58.359 --> 00:00:59.359
Graffiti.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:02.960
“It was
all like an unknown world to me.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.220
In a way, but it was also super
exciting.“

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.130
Jane Golden founded Philly’s mural arts
program.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:13.250
She came to Philly from LA, where she’d
painted more than 50 murals.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.711
She was hired in the 1980s in to work as a
part of Mayor Wilson Goode’s “Anti-Graffiti

00:01:17.711 --> 00:01:19.000
Network.”

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.659
People saw graffiti as a plague.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:26.890
So the idea was to stop graffiti by recruiting
graffiti artists to make murals.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:31.251
“If you had met me back then, my colleagues
would have been Baby Rock, and Knife, and

00:01:31.251 --> 00:01:34.159
Cool Earl, and Disco Duck.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:36.610
It was unbelievable, it was so great.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:41.770
I like fell in love with Philadelphia through
the eyes of young people and community leaders.”

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.420
Here’s Dr. J. in a suit.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.600
He looks good, right?

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:52.590
When this mural was painted in 1990, people
were certain it’d be defaced with graffiti.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:58.509
When it wasn’t, it proved an anti-graffiti
strategy — murals could serve as an artistic

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:00.369
shield against vandalism.

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:04.039
That’s become a model for lots of cities
nationwide.

00:02:04.039 --> 00:02:09.440
“At the end of the day, it was like the
perfect vehicle to really move these kids

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:15.030
from where they were to another point, without
being like a fascist or a dictator or too

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.080
prescriptive.”

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.910
But for Philly to grow into a city with thousands
of murals, it took a total transformation—

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:26.810
not just for the city, but for the program
that covered it, too.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.170
Murals need money.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.530
Mural Arts scrambles to get it.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:34.950
That was the case with Common Threads, when
Meg Saligman approached Jane Golden with an

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:35.950
idea.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.270
“She said, do you have funding?”

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:41.410
“And I was like, mmm, not really,” I said,
“but I’ll find funding.”

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:42.410
“She said ‘really?’

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.610
and I was like, ‘yeah,’ so she said, ‘OK,
guess what, I got a grant from the National

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:46.610
Endowment of the Arts.’

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.290
It was a grant for however much money.”

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.310
“So I said, OK, that’ll be our start.”

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:56.861
“So then I went and I found somebody from
the city, so we put that in the pot.”

00:02:56.861 --> 00:03:01.050
“Then we applied to a few little foundations
and we got some grants, and then we kept raising

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.050
money.”

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.151
“And we’d run out of money, and it’d
be like halfway.

00:03:03.151 --> 00:03:04.151
And we’d be like,

00:03:04.151 --> 00:03:05.800
oh my God, we better raise some more money!”

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.390
And I remember we got further down and further
down the wall, and then finally, I remember

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.380
the guy who came, he was working for the Philadelphia
Foundation, he had a check for us for $5,000,

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.970
and he drove up to the wall
and he goes, “Here’s your check!”

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:19.490
And it allowed us to finish the mural.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:25.200
That scramble was only possible because in
the mid-90s, Mural Arts restructured to use

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.800
both public and private money.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:33.020
The organization brings in an average of $1.50
in private funds for every dollar it gets

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:34.020
from the city.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:39.230
“I think our key ingredient is the leveraging
of public and private funds, because without

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:44.790
both sectors at work, we would do less work,
and I think having the long term support of

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:48.730
the city has ensured our longevity.”

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:53.000
That means it gets city perks, like the historic
building that’s home to the program.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.380
But it also seeks private dollars to pay for
artists and materials.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.960
And that money doesn’t just change a city’s
colors.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.110
It changes its character, too.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:12.280
Let’s say you don’t care about Theatre
of Life on Lombard and Broad or

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:14.930
One World just over on 16th.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.660
The walls are just part of the program.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.660
Murals can boost property values and combat
blight.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:21.870
That Common Threads building?

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:26.300
Today it’s being marketed as the “Mural
Lofts.”

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.039
People see those murals, too.

00:04:28.039 --> 00:04:34.370
In 2014, tours of the city brought
15,000 people to see the murals, with 65%

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:37.610
leaving the center of the city to see more
neighborhoods.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.370
Across the street from Common Threads, you’ll
find a mural on a school.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.479
In addition to employing a staff of artists,
Mural Arts includes education programs for

00:04:45.479 --> 00:04:47.500
about 1,000 students.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:52.430
Mural Arts also designs some programs specifically
for artists with mental health and substance

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.060
abuse challenges.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.760
The Yale School of Medicine found that this
“public art” helped “public health.”

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:03.699
And the Mural Arts Guild program, for formerly
incarcerated individuals, reduced the rate

00:05:03.699 --> 00:05:04.900
of return to prison.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.389
The laundry list of benefits is impressive.

00:05:07.389 --> 00:05:12.900
But these murals are really about communities
expressing themselves, whether it’s a

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.479
photo-realistic picture of a legendary pool
player,

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:19.729
Or a modern collage featuring icons like The
Roots.

00:05:19.729 --> 00:05:26.300
The goal is to help communities make the city
their city, whether they like Shepard Fairey,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.060
or Keith Haring, or Frank Sinatra.

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:34.620
“What we want is for the community to be
ok with it, right?

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.449
To be inspired by it, so that it has meaning
for them.”

00:05:38.449 --> 00:05:43.780
“And then all of it together, it’s like
this giant crescendo, that really speaks to

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:48.160
the importance of
not just beauty— but beauty does have importance—

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.330
but also representation, and how that representation
happens.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.430
It can happen all different ways.”

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:54.740
“How great is that?

00:05:54.740 --> 00:06:00.060
That we get to experience all these different
narratives that, in the end,

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:01.060
speak about our lives?

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.939
But isn’t that what art does, that it shines
a light on our

00:06:04.939 --> 00:06:07.790
diversity, but lifts up our commonality and
in the end

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:09.779
connects us to all that makes us human?

00:06:09.779 --> 00:06:15.259
I think that at the end of the day that probably
inspires me almost more than anything.”

00:06:15.259 --> 00:06:18.629
“I am a very serious wallhunter.

00:06:18.629 --> 00:06:24.210
This means that no matter where I am, no matter
what city I am in, I will notice really good

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:25.289
blank walls.”

